0: //! A key-value store optimized for atomically committing a small collection of metadata.
1: //!
2: //! [Metadata] is a key-value store optimized for tracking a small collection of metadata
3: //! that allows multiple updates to be committed in a single batch. It is commonly used with
4: //! a variety of other underlying storage systems to persist application state across restarts.
5: //!
6: //! # Format
7: //!
8: //! Data stored in [Metadata] is serialized as a sequence of key-value pairs in either a
9: //! "left" or "right" blob:
10: //!
11: //! ```text
12: //! +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
13: //! | 0 | 1 |    ...    | 8 | 9 |10 |11 |12 |13 |14 |15 |16 |  ...  |50 |...|90 |91 |92 |93 |
14: //! +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
15: //! |    Version (u64)  |      Key1     |              Value1           |...|  CRC32(u32)   |
16: //! +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
17: //! ```
18: //!
19: //! _To ensure the integrity of the data, a CRC32 checksum is appended to the end of the blob.
20: //! This ensures that partial writes are detected before any data is relied on._
21: //!
22: //! # Atomic Updates
23: //!
24: //! To provide support for atomic updates, [Metadata] maintains two blobs: a "left" and a "right"
25: //! blob. When a new update is committed, it is written to the "older" of the two blobs (indicated
26: //! by the version persisted). Writes to [commonware_runtime::Blob] are not atomic and may only
27: //! complete partially, so we only overwrite the "newer" blob once the "older" blob has been synced
28: //! (otherwise, we would not be guaranteed to recover the latest complete state from disk on
29: //! restart as half of a blob could be old data and half new data).
30: //!
31: //! # Delta Writes
32: //!
33: //! If the set of keys and the length of values are stable, [Metadata] will only write an update's
34: //! delta to disk (rather than rewriting the entire metadata). This makes [Metadata] a great choice
35: //! for maintaining even large collections of data (with the majority rarely modified).
36: //!
37: //! # Example
38: //!
39: //! ```rust
40: //! use commonware_runtime::{Spawner, Runner, deterministic};
41: //! use commonware_storage::metadata::{Metadata, Config};
42: //! use commonware_utils::sequence::U64;
43: //!
44: //! let executor = deterministic::Runner::default();
45: //! executor.start(|context| async move {
46: //!     // Create a store
47: //!     let mut metadata = Metadata::init(context, Config {
48: //!         partition: "partition".into(),
49: //!         codec_config: ((0..).into(), ()),
50: //!     }).await.unwrap();
51: //!
52: //!     // Store metadata
53: //!     metadata.put(U64::new(1), b"hello".to_vec());
54: //!     metadata.put(U64::new(2), b"world".to_vec());
55: //!
56: //!     // Sync the metadata store (batch write changes)
57: //!     metadata = metadata.sync().await.unwrap();
58: //!
59: //!     // Retrieve some metadata
60: //!     let value = metadata.get(&U64::new(1)).unwrap();
61: //!
62: //! });
63: //! ```
